## ***********************************************************************
## **
## **  GUI.INC.BL -- three dialogs, in a box that puts the screen back.
## **
## **      GUI.YN      a question, and a Y or N answer
## **      GUI.MENU    a question, and a row chosen from a list
## **      GUI.TEXT    a question, and a line of typed text
## **
## **  What they share is the BOX: measure it against the text, centre it, save
## **  what it covers, draw a frame, give the pixels back on the way out. That is
## **  GUI.OPEN and GUI.CLOSE, and the three entry points are thin. GUI.MENU is
## **  MENUHELP.RUN inside a frame and GUI.TEXT is INPHELP.GET inside one, so
## **  only GUI.YN -- four keys -- reads the keyboard itself.
## **
## **  REQUIRES, and does not #INCLUDE for you, in this order:
## **      GPB.INC.BL        GP.BOX, GP.FILL, GP.PRINTAT, GP.CALL
## **      STASH.INC.BL      saves the cells the box covers -- and wants a
## **                        #SYMFILE in your program, see its own header
## **      THEME.INC.BL      the colour roles, and GOSUB THEME.LOAD first
## **      MENUHELP.INC.BL   GUI.MENU only
## **      INPHELP.INC.BL    GUI.TEXT only
## **
## **  Usage, the whole of it:
## **      THEME.DARK = 0 : GOSUB THEME.LOAD
## **      GUI.BANK = 8
## **      GUI.MSG$ = "Delete the file?"
## **      GOSUB GUI.YN
## **      IF GUI.ANSWER = 1 THEN <yes>
## **
## **   in   GUI.MSG$        the question. "" for none
## **        GUI.MSG2$       a second line, for detail that does not fit. "" for
## **                        none, and then there is no gap
## **        GUI.TITLE$      a name in the top edge of the frame. "" for none
## **        GUI.BANK        a spare RAM bank to save the covered cells in.
## **                        0 MEANS DO NOT SAVE, and then the box is still on
## **                        the screen when the call returns
## **        GUI.STYLE       0, the default, draws the single line box. 1 to 5
## **                        are GP.BOX's other styles
## **        GUI.PANEL.IN    the panel: the box's background AND the colour the
## **                        message lines are written in. 0 takes THEME.TEXT
## **        GUI.BORDER.IN   the frame's attribute. 0 takes THEME.BORDER
## **        GUI.GLYPH       non-zero frames the box from six glyphs of the
## **                        CALLER'S, and GUI.STYLE is then not consulted.
## **                        They are TILE INDICES: GUI.FRAME hands them to
## **                        GP.BOX as a custom set, which does not convert
## **                            GUI.EDGE.H      the horizontal run
## **                            GUI.EDGE.V      the vertical run
## **                            GUI.CORNER.TL   .TR  .BL  .BR
## **        GUI.PLACE       0 centres the box. Non-zero uses GUI.X, GUI.Y
## **        GUI.X  GUI.Y    top left, when GUI.PLACE says so
## **        GUI.ROW.OFFSET  added to the row the box lands on, however it was
## **                        placed. 0 for almost everybody -- see below
## **
## **   out  GUI.KEY         the key that ended it, whichever call
## **        GUI.LEFT GUI.TOP GUI.WIDTH GUI.HEIGHT   where the box went, and
## **                        how big it turned out
## **
## **  GUI.ROW.OFFSET IS FOR HARDWARE SCROLLING. The GP commands address the text
## **  MAP, and VERA's L1_VSCROLL decides which map row shows as screen row 0. A
## **  caller scrolling that way sets GUI.ROW.OFFSET to VSCROLL/8 and is centred
## **  against what the user can see. Everyone else leaves it 0.
## **
## **  UPPER CASE, ON THE DEFAULT CHARSET. GP.PRINTAT converts PETSCII and
## **  BASLOAD passes literals through as source bytes, so on charset 2 ASCII
## **  lower case lands on the graphics half of the font -- "Confirm" measured as
## **  tiles 3, 79, 78, 70, 73, 82, 77. ISO mode fixes the text and breaks the
## **  frame, GP.BOX's $40-$7D being letters in that order: so it is upper case
## **  with a frame, or mixed case without one, unless the caller re-orders the
## **  font and says where the glyphs went with GUI.GLYPH. That is what
## **  samples/editor does, and the whole reason the frame is overridable.
## **
## **  A BOX OVER 4094 CELLS WILL NOT FIT ONE BANK, and that is not an error: the
## **  save is skipped, the dialog still runs, and GUI.STASHED comes back 0 so the
## **  caller knows it must repaint. Same when GUI.BANK is 0.
## **
## **  .IN IS "WHAT THE CALLER ASKED FOR" -- GUI.PANEL and GUI.BORDER are what
## **  GUI.OPEN settled on, and those are what everything downstream draws with.
## **  Both requests are 0-means-default: attribute 0 is black on black, legal and
## **  no use to anybody, so it is free to spend as "I did not choose".
## **
## **  The whole GUI.* name space belongs to this file.
## **
## ***********************************************************************
#IFNDEF GUI.DEFS
#DEFINE GUI.DEFS 1

## screen_mode. With carry SET it REPORTS rather than sets: mode in A, columns in
## X, rows in Y. Named here so GUI does not depend on APPHELP being included.
#DEFINE GUI.SCRMODE $FF5F

## The frame GUI.STYLE 0 means. GP.BOX style 2 is the single line.
#DEFINE GUI.LINEBOX 2

## A bank is 8K and a cell 2 bytes, so this is the ceiling on a saved rectangle.
#DEFINE GUI.MAXCELLS 4094

## Air INSIDE the frame: columns each side, rows above and below. One place to
## change, and every box changes with it.
#DEFINE GUI.PADX 2
#DEFINE GUI.PADY 1

## Keys GUI.YN answers to. Y and N without case, and both ways of saying "no
## thanks" -- ESC and STOP -- which answer NO rather than hanging.
#DEFINE GUI.UY 89
#DEFINE GUI.LY 121
#DEFINE GUI.UN 78
#DEFINE GUI.LN 110

## ...and the other pair, for GUI.OKCANCEL. O and C.
#DEFINE GUI.UO 79
#DEFINE GUI.LO 111
#DEFINE GUI.UC 67
#DEFINE GUI.LC 99
#DEFINE GUI.ESCAPE 27
#DEFINE GUI.STOP 3
#DEFINE GUI.RETURN 13
#DEFINE GUI.SPACE 32

GOTO GUI.MODULE.END

## ***********************************************************************
## **  GUI.YN -- a question, and a Y or N answer.
## **
## **   in   GUI.MSG$ GUI.MSG2$ GUI.TITLE$ and the box inputs
## **        GUI.HINT$   the answers, under the question. "" takes the default,
## **                    "YES      NO". Whatever it says, the first Y and the
## **                    first N in it are lit as the keys
## **        GUI.OKCANCEL  0 asks YES/NO on Y and N, the default. Non-zero asks
## **                    OK/CANCEL on O and C, and then RETURN also accepts
## **
## **   out  GUI.ANSWER  1 for yes or OK, 0 for no or cancel
## **        GUI.KEY     the key itself, so a caller that cares whether the no
## **                    was ESC or N can still tell
## **
## **  ESC AND STOP ANSWER NO. A dialog that can only be answered can trap
## **  someone, and "no" is the safe reading of walking away from a question.
## ***********************************************************************

GUI.YN:

## ---- which pair of answers, and therefore which two keys. Everything below
## ---- works off GUI.YES/GUI.NO, so the two modes differ in four assignments and
## ---- a default string rather than in two routines.
    GUI.YES = GUI.UY : GUI.YES.ALT = GUI.LY
    GUI.NO = GUI.UN : GUI.NO.ALT = GUI.LN
    GUI.HINT.DEFAULT$ = "YES      NO"
    IF GUI.OKCANCEL = 0 THEN GOTO GUI.YN.SET
    GUI.YES = GUI.UO : GUI.YES.ALT = GUI.LO
    GUI.NO = GUI.UC : GUI.NO.ALT = GUI.LC
    GUI.HINT.DEFAULT$ = "OK      CANCEL"

GUI.YN.SET:
    GUI.HINT.LINE$ = GUI.HINT$
    IF GUI.HINT.LINE$ = "" THEN GUI.HINT.LINE$ = GUI.HINT.DEFAULT$
    GUI.BODY.ROWS = 1 : GUI.BODY.WIDTH = LEN(GUI.HINT.LINE$)
    GOSUB GUI.OPEN
    GUI.HINT.LEFT = GUI.INNER.LEFT + INT((GUI.INNER.WIDTH - LEN(GUI.HINT.LINE$)) / 2)
    IF GUI.HINT.LEFT < GUI.INNER.LEFT THEN GUI.HINT.LEFT = GUI.INNER.LEFT
    GP.PRINTAT GUI.HINT.LEFT, GUI.INNER.TOP, GUI.HINT.LINE$, THEME.CLR(THEME.DIMMED)
    GUI.MARK.CH = GUI.YES : GOSUB GUI.YN.MARK
    GUI.MARK.CH = GUI.NO : GOSUB GUI.YN.MARK
    GUI.ANSWER = 0

## ---- Six keys scattered across 256 with everything else falling through: the
## ---- sparse selector GP.SELECT is for, and the comma lists are what earn it --
## ---- "yes, or the lower case yes" is one alternative and reads as one.
## ---- Costs no runtime: all four select keywords are markers on a core handler,
## ---- so this does not drag the GP block into a program that has none.

GUI.YN.WAIT:
    GET GUI.PRESSED$
    IF GUI.PRESSED$ = "" THEN GOTO GUI.YN.WAIT
    GUI.KEY = ASC(GUI.PRESSED$) : GUI.DONE = 0
    GP.SELECT GUI.KEY
        GP.CASE GUI.YES, GUI.YES.ALT
            GUI.ANSWER = 1 : GUI.DONE = 1
        GP.CASE GUI.NO, GUI.NO.ALT, GUI.ESCAPE, GUI.STOP
            GUI.DONE = 1

## ---- RETURN accepts, but ONLY in OK/CANCEL. A dialog offering OK has a default
## ---- and RETURN is how everyone takes it; a YES/NO question is asked precisely
## ---- because there is none, and it is usually the destructive one. In YES/NO
## ---- the key falls out with GUI.DONE still 0, which is what ignoring it means.
        GP.CASE GUI.RETURN
            IF GUI.OKCANCEL <> 0 THEN GUI.ANSWER = 1 : GUI.DONE = 1
    GP.ENDSEL
    IF GUI.DONE = 0 THEN GOTO GUI.YN.WAIT

GUI.YN.DONE:
    GOSUB GUI.CLOSE : RETURN

## ***********************************************************************
## **  GUI.YN.MARK -- repaint one letter of the hint in the key colour.
## **
## **   in   GUI.MARK.CH    the character code to look for
## **        GUI.HINT.LINE$ GUI.HINT.LEFT GUI.INNER.TOP   the line as printed
## **
## **  THE FIRST MATCH WINS, so a key's letter has to be the first of its kind in
## **  the line -- which is why "YES      NO" and "OK      CANCEL" are in that
## **  order. A hint string that puts an N before the NO lights the wrong cell.
## ***********************************************************************

GUI.YN.MARK:
    FOR GUI.INDEX = 1 TO LEN(GUI.HINT.LINE$)
        IF ASC(MID$(GUI.HINT.LINE$, GUI.INDEX, 1)) = GUI.MARK.CH THEN GP.PRINTAT GUI.HINT.LEFT + GUI.INDEX - 1, GUI.INNER.TOP, MID$(GUI.HINT.LINE$, GUI.INDEX, 1), THEME.CLR(THEME.TITLE) : RETURN
    NEXT GUI.INDEX
    RETURN

## ***********************************************************************
## **  GUI.MENU -- a question, and a row chosen from a list.
## **
## **   in   GUI.COUNT          how many rows
## **        MENUHELP.ITEM$()    the rows, 1..GUI.COUNT
## **        GUI.SEL             the row to start on, 0 for the first
## **        GUI.FLAGS           passed to MENUHELP.FLAGS -- MUSTSEL,
## **                            NOWRAP, GAMEPAD. KEEPMARK is meaningless
## **                            here because the box does not survive
## **
## **   out  GUI.SEL             1..GUI.COUNT, or 0 if cancelled
## **        GUI.KEY             13 chose, 27 cancelled
## **
## **  THE ROWS GO IN MENUHELP.ITEM$ AND THE CALLER DIMS IT -- MENUHELP's own
## **  contract. A second array here would copy every row on every call and still
## **  have to guess a bound, which is the thing MENUHELP refuses to do.
## **
## **  Items are NOT indented here: MENUHELP prints them at the left edge of the
## **  highlight, so a leading space in the item is what insets the bar.
## ***********************************************************************

GUI.MENU:
    GUI.BODY.ROWS = GUI.COUNT
    GUI.BODY.WIDTH = 0
    FOR GUI.INDEX = 1 TO GUI.COUNT
        IF LEN(MENUHELP.ITEM$(GUI.INDEX)) > GUI.BODY.WIDTH THEN GUI.BODY.WIDTH = LEN(MENUHELP.ITEM$(GUI.INDEX))
    NEXT GUI.INDEX
    GOSUB GUI.OPEN
    MENUHELP.X = GUI.INNER.LEFT : MENUHELP.Y = GUI.INNER.TOP
    MENUHELP.WIDTH = GUI.INNER.WIDTH : MENUHELP.COUNT = GUI.COUNT
    REM GUI.PANEL, the panel GUI.OPEN just painted, so unselected rows are the colour of
    REM the box they sit in. THEME.PAGE was blue on blue in the light palette -- fine for a
    REM GP.FILL, where only the background shows, invisible for anything with letters.
    MENUHELP.ATTR = GUI.PANEL : MENUHELP.HIATTR = THEME.CLR(THEME.HILITE)
    MENUHELP.HOTATTR = 0 : MENUHELP.FLAGS = GUI.FLAGS : MENUHELP.SEL = GUI.SEL
    GOSUB MENUHELP.RUN
    GUI.SEL = MENUHELP.SEL : GUI.KEY = MENUHELP.KEY
    GOSUB GUI.CLOSE
    RETURN

## ***********************************************************************
## **  GUI.TEXT -- a question, and a line of typed text.
## **
## **   in   GUI.LEN     how many characters fit. 0 takes 20
## **        GUI.INPUT$  the starting value, edited in place
## **        GUI.MASK    non-zero shows asterisks, for a password
## **
## **   out  GUI.INPUT$  what was typed
## **        GUI.OK      1 if RETURN finished it, 0 if it was cancelled
## **        GUI.KEY     the key itself
## **
## **  THE RESULT IS GUI.INPUT$, NOT GUI.TEXT$: GUI.TEXT is this routine's label,
## **  and a string of the same name beside it reads fine and compiles into
## **  something else. The field is drawn in THEME.HILITE so it is visibly a place
## **  to type, and INPHELP restores the old text on ESC.
## **
## **  THE HINT LINE IS FIXED HERE, unlike GUI.YN's: its two key names are painted
## **  at known offsets, so a caller's string would highlight the wrong words.
## **
## **  Cursor up, down and TAB do NOT leave this dialog -- with one field there is
## **  nowhere to move to. Only RETURN and ESC end it.
## ***********************************************************************

GUI.TEXT:
    IF GUI.LEN = 0 THEN GUI.LEN = 20
    GUI.HINT.LINE$ = "ENTER = OK    ESC = CANCEL"
    GUI.BODY.ROWS = 3 : GUI.BODY.WIDTH = GUI.LEN
    IF LEN(GUI.HINT.LINE$) > GUI.BODY.WIDTH THEN GUI.BODY.WIDTH = LEN(GUI.HINT.LINE$)
    GUI.WAS$ = GUI.INPUT$
    GOSUB GUI.OPEN

## ---- the field, centred over the inner width like the hint under it
    GUI.FIELD.LEFT = GUI.INNER.LEFT + INT((GUI.INNER.WIDTH - GUI.LEN) / 2)
    IF GUI.FIELD.LEFT < GUI.INNER.LEFT THEN GUI.FIELD.LEFT = GUI.INNER.LEFT
    INPHELP.X = GUI.FIELD.LEFT : INPHELP.Y = GUI.INNER.TOP
    INPHELP.LEN = GUI.LEN : INPHELP.ATTR = THEME.CLR(THEME.HILITE)
    INPHELP.TEXT$ = GUI.INPUT$ : INPHELP.MASK = GUI.MASK

## ---- the two key names picked out of the dimmed line, at known offsets rather
## ---- than searched for, because unlike GUI.YN's hint this text is fixed.
    GUI.HINT.LEFT = GUI.INNER.LEFT + INT((GUI.INNER.WIDTH - LEN(GUI.HINT.LINE$)) / 2)
    IF GUI.HINT.LEFT < GUI.INNER.LEFT THEN GUI.HINT.LEFT = GUI.INNER.LEFT
    GP.PRINTAT GUI.HINT.LEFT, GUI.INNER.TOP + 2, GUI.HINT.LINE$, THEME.CLR(THEME.DIMMED)
    GP.PRINTAT GUI.HINT.LEFT, GUI.INNER.TOP + 2, "ENTER", THEME.CLR(THEME.TITLE)
    GP.PRINTAT GUI.HINT.LEFT + 14, GUI.INNER.TOP + 2, "ESC", THEME.CLR(THEME.TITLE)

## ---- UP, DOWN AND TAB LEAVE AN INPHELP FIELD WITHOUT CANCELLING IT -- that is
## ---- how a form moves between fields. With one field they came back as "not
## ---- RETURN", read as a cancel, and an arrow key silently threw the typing
## ---- away. Re-enter the field instead: only the two named keys end this.
GUI.TEXT.FIELD:
    GOSUB INPHELP.GET
    IF INPHELP.KEY = INPHELP.DOWN THEN GOTO GUI.TEXT.FIELD
    IF INPHELP.KEY = INPHELP.UP THEN GOTO GUI.TEXT.FIELD
    IF INPHELP.KEY = INPHELP.TAB THEN GOTO GUI.TEXT.FIELD

    GUI.INPUT$ = INPHELP.TEXT$
    GUI.KEY = INPHELP.KEY
    GUI.OK = 0
    IF GUI.KEY = GUI.RETURN THEN GUI.OK = 1

## ---- A cancel gives back what was passed IN. INPHELP restores to the value at
## ---- ITS last call, which after an arrow key is not the same thing.
    IF GUI.OK = 0 THEN GUI.INPUT$ = GUI.WAS$
    GOSUB GUI.CLOSE
    RETURN

## ***********************************************************************
## **  GUI.OPEN -- measure, place, save, draw, and print the question.
## **
## **   in   GUI.BODY.ROWS   how many rows the caller needs under the message
## **        GUI.BODY.WIDTH  and how wide its widest one is
## **
## **   out  GUI.INNER.LEFT GUI.INNER.TOP   where the caller's own rows start
## **        GUI.INNER.WIDTH          how wide they may be
## **        GUI.STASHED     1 if the covered cells were saved
## **
## **  Public: a caller wanting a fourth kind of dialog should build it on this
## **  rather than on a copy of it.
## ***********************************************************************

GUI.OPEN:
    GOSUB GUI.SIZE : GOSUB GUI.PLACE.BOX

## ---- Save what the box is about to cover. Both reasons to skip are ordinary
## ---- rather than errors, and both leave GUI.STASHED at 0: no bank offered, or
## ---- a rectangle bigger than a bank holds.

    GUI.STASHED = 0
    IF GUI.BANK = 0 THEN GOTO GUI.OPEN.DRAW
    IF GUI.WIDTH * GUI.HEIGHT > GUI.MAXCELLS THEN GOTO GUI.OPEN.DRAW
    STASH.BANK = GUI.BANK
    STASH.X = GUI.LEFT : STASH.Y = GUI.TOP : STASH.W = GUI.WIDTH : STASH.H = GUI.HEIGHT
    GOSUB STASH.SAVE
    GUI.STASHED = STASH.OK

## ---- Fill THEN frame: the fill is what makes the box opaque. Without it the
## ---- frame is a rectangle over whatever was underneath, and reads as a mistake.

GUI.OPEN.DRAW:
    GUI.BOX.STYLE = GUI.STYLE
    IF GUI.BOX.STYLE = 0 THEN GUI.BOX.STYLE = GUI.LINEBOX
## ---- What the caller asked for, or the theme, settled ONCE here so the fill,
## ---- the frame, the message lines and MENUHELP's rows all read one answer.
    GUI.PANEL = GUI.PANEL.IN
    IF GUI.PANEL = 0 THEN GUI.PANEL = THEME.CLR(THEME.TEXT)
    GUI.BORDER = GUI.BORDER.IN
    IF GUI.BORDER = 0 THEN GUI.BORDER = THEME.CLR(THEME.BORDER)
    GP.FILL GUI.LEFT, GUI.TOP, GUI.WIDTH, GUI.HEIGHT, GUI.SPACE, GUI.PANEL
    IF GUI.GLYPH <> 0 THEN GOSUB GUI.FRAME : GOTO GUI.OPEN.TITLE
    GP.BOX GUI.LEFT, GUI.TOP, GUI.WIDTH, GUI.HEIGHT, GUI.BOX.STYLE, GUI.BORDER
GUI.OPEN.TITLE:
    IF GUI.TITLE$ <> "" THEN GUI.TITLE.LEFT = GUI.LEFT + INT((GUI.WIDTH - LEN(GUI.TITLE$) - 2) / 2) : GP.PRINTAT GUI.TITLE.LEFT, GUI.TOP, " " + GUI.TITLE$ + " ", THEME.CLR(THEME.TITLE)

## ---- The question, then a blank row before the caller's own -- but only if
## ---- there WAS a question. A menu with no message opens with no empty line.

    GUI.INNER.LEFT = GUI.LEFT + 1 + GUI.PADX : GUI.INNER.TOP = GUI.TOP + 1 + GUI.PADY
    IF GUI.MSG$ <> "" THEN GP.PRINTAT GUI.INNER.LEFT, GUI.INNER.TOP, GUI.MSG$, GUI.PANEL : GUI.INNER.TOP = GUI.INNER.TOP + 1
    IF GUI.MSG2$ <> "" THEN GP.PRINTAT GUI.INNER.LEFT, GUI.INNER.TOP, GUI.MSG2$, GUI.PANEL : GUI.INNER.TOP = GUI.INNER.TOP + 1
    IF GUI.HEAD.ROWS > 0 THEN GUI.INNER.TOP = GUI.INNER.TOP + 1
    RETURN

## ***********************************************************************
## **  GUI.FRAME -- the border, from the caller's own six glyphs.
## **
## **   in   GUI.LEFT GUI.TOP GUI.WIDTH GUI.HEIGHT   the rectangle
## **        GUI.EDGE.H .. GUI.CORNER.BR             the six glyphs
## **        GUI.BORDER                              their attribute
## **   out  GUI.RIGHT GUI.BOTTOM                    the far column and row
## **
## **  THE GLYPHS ARE TILE INDICES, not PETSCII: GP.BOX writes a custom set
## **  straight to the cell. GP.FILL converts, and converts even with the $0372
## **  ASCII flag set -- which is why the eight fills this replaced needed their
## **  glyphs biased by 64.
## ***********************************************************************

GUI.FRAME:
    IF GUI.WIDTH < 2 THEN RETURN
    IF GUI.HEIGHT < 2 THEN RETURN
    GUI.RIGHT = GUI.LEFT + GUI.WIDTH - 1
    GUI.BOTTOM = GUI.TOP + GUI.HEIGHT - 1
    IF LEN(GUI.GLYPH$) <> 8 THEN GOSUB GUI.GLYPHS
    GP.BOX GUI.LEFT, GUI.TOP, GUI.WIDTH, GUI.HEIGHT, GP.STRPTR(GUI.GLYPH$) + 1, GUI.BORDER
    RETURN

## ***********************************************************************
## **  GUI.GLYPHS -- the six, packed into the eight bytes GP.BOX reads.
## **
## **  The order is TR TL BR BL TOP BOTTOM LEFT RIGHT. Cached, because a string
## **  built per dialog is garbage per dialog -- set GUI.GLYPH$ = "" to force a
## **  rebuild after changing a glyph. The POINTER is taken fresh at every GP.BOX
## **  above, since a string moves when BASIC tidies.
## ***********************************************************************

GUI.GLYPHS:
    GUI.GLYPH$ = CHR$(GUI.CORNER.TR) + CHR$(GUI.CORNER.TL) + CHR$(GUI.CORNER.BR) + CHR$(GUI.CORNER.BL)
    GUI.GLYPH$ = GUI.GLYPH$ + CHR$(GUI.EDGE.H) + CHR$(GUI.EDGE.H) + CHR$(GUI.EDGE.V) + CHR$(GUI.EDGE.V)
    RETURN

## ***********************************************************************
## **  GUI.CLOSE -- give the pixels back, if we took a copy.
## ***********************************************************************

GUI.CLOSE:
    IF GUI.STASHED = 0 THEN RETURN
    STASH.BANK = GUI.BANK : STASH.MOVE = 0 : GOSUB STASH.RESTORE
    RETURN

## ***********************************************************************
## **  GUI.SIZE -- how big the box has to be.
## **
## **  Width is the widest thing that must fit; height is the message, a blank,
## **  the caller's rows and the two frame rows. The 4 on the width is two frame
## **  columns and a column of air each side.
## ***********************************************************************

GUI.SIZE:
    GUI.HEAD.ROWS = 0
    IF GUI.MSG$ <> "" THEN GUI.HEAD.ROWS = 1
    IF GUI.MSG2$ <> "" THEN GUI.HEAD.ROWS = 2
    GUI.INNER.WIDTH = GUI.BODY.WIDTH
    IF LEN(GUI.MSG$) > GUI.INNER.WIDTH THEN GUI.INNER.WIDTH = LEN(GUI.MSG$)
    IF LEN(GUI.MSG2$) > GUI.INNER.WIDTH THEN GUI.INNER.WIDTH = LEN(GUI.MSG2$)
    IF LEN(GUI.TITLE$) + 2 > GUI.INNER.WIDTH THEN GUI.INNER.WIDTH = LEN(GUI.TITLE$) + 2
    GUI.GAP.ROWS = 0 : IF GUI.HEAD.ROWS > 0 THEN GUI.GAP.ROWS = 1
    GUI.WIDTH = GUI.INNER.WIDTH + GUI.PADX * 2 + 2
    GUI.HEIGHT = GUI.HEAD.ROWS + GUI.GAP.ROWS + GUI.BODY.ROWS + GUI.PADY * 2 + 2
    RETURN

## ***********************************************************************
## **  GUI.PLACE.BOX -- centred, or where the caller said.
## **
## **  Clamped at 0 on both axes, so a box too big for the mode is cut off at the
## **  right and bottom rather than drawn at a negative coordinate -- which VERA
## **  would happily write somewhere else entirely.
## ***********************************************************************

GUI.PLACE.BOX:
    GOSUB GUI.SCREEN
    IF GUI.PLACE <> 0 THEN GUI.LEFT = GUI.X : GUI.TOP = GUI.Y : GOTO GUI.PLACE.SCROLL
    GUI.LEFT = INT((GUI.SCREEN.COLS - GUI.WIDTH) / 2)
    GUI.TOP = INT((GUI.SCREEN.ROWS - GUI.HEIGHT) / 2)
    IF GUI.LEFT < 0 THEN GUI.LEFT = 0
    IF GUI.TOP < 0 THEN GUI.TOP = 0

## ---- LAST, and on BOTH paths: from here on every coordinate -- the frame, the
## ---- text, the rectangle STASH saves -- is a map row. Applying it to the
## ---- explicit path too is what keeps GUI.X and GUI.Y screen coordinates.
GUI.PLACE.SCROLL:
    GUI.TOP = GUI.TOP + GUI.ROW.OFFSET
    RETURN

## ***********************************************************************
## **  GUI.SCREEN -- the mode's size, asked rather than assumed.
## **
## **  NOT CACHED: a program that switches mode between two dialogs would place the
## **  second against the first one's screen. It is one KERNAL call on a path that
## **  is about to wait for a human. The floors catch a mode that reports something
## **  unusable, and 80x60 is a safe thing to be wrong towards.
## ***********************************************************************

GUI.SCREEN:
    GP.CALL GUI.SCRMODE, 0, 0, 0, 1
    GUI.SCREEN.COLS = GP.X : GUI.SCREEN.ROWS = GP.Y
    IF GUI.SCREEN.COLS < 20 THEN GUI.SCREEN.COLS = 80
    IF GUI.SCREEN.ROWS < 8 THEN GUI.SCREEN.ROWS = 60
    RETURN

GUI.MODULE.END:
#ENDIF
